297:
(a) The establishment of an enforcing underage drinking law coalition between the City of Albany and Dougherty County is hereby approved and authorized.
(b) The Albany Police Department will be the lead agency in a county-wide collaborative effort to request funds for addressing the issue of underage drinking in Dougherty County. The City of Albany will be the fiscal agent for any grant funds received.
(c) Upon receipt of grant funds, the city police department, the county sheriff's office, and the county police department, along with the district attorney of Dougherty County's office will work together to implement any grant funds received. The Albany Dougherty Drug Unit will coordinate the law enforcement efforts.
(d) The objectives of the coalition will be to:
(1) Provide for educational and public awareness campaigns and organizational activities;
(2) Develop comprehensive strategic plans based on local needs assessments which include input from law enforcement, youth, vendors, community leaders and parents;
(3) Develop uniform local data collection systems to gather information on violations of state and local alcohol laws and regulations;
(4) Institute more effective local laws and regulations governing alcohol beverage industry sales, advertising and licensing;
(5) Establish youth coalitions or other youth led initiatives to assist in the development and implementation of local strategies;
(6) Provide additional resources to law enforcement agencies allowing them to expand the number of undercover compliance checks aimed at local alcohol vendors;
(7) Provide responsible alcohol service training and technical assistance to vendors and their employees to assist them in curtailing illegal sales to underage persons;
(8) To coordinate all local activities through statewide policy development and public education/awareness campaign.
(Res. No. 02-033, §§ 1—4, 8-5-02)
Editor's note— Res. No. 02-033, §§ 1—4, adopted Aug. 5, 2002, pertained to the establishment of and enforcing underage drinking law coalition. Such resolution did not specify manner of codification; hence, inclusion as § 2-3-4 was at the discretion of the editor.

